  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1240 AM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nThursday through Tuesday:\n\nSynopsis: Warm, humid, and unsettled weather is expected to remain \nin place through much of the extended forecast, with a slight \nsignal on some ECMWF/GEFS ensemble plots for a cooling trend \n(partial breakdown of the upper ridge) late this weekend into \nearly next week. The crest of the upper ridge should remain over\nor just north of the state, with occasional ripples in the \npattern yielding chances for showers and thunderstorms. \n\nThursday AM: Though the nose of the LLJ is rather broad across \nsouthern WI predawn through late Thursday morning, ripples in the \n700-500mb flow could aid in some convective initiation, and it's \nsafe to say there is a chance (35-60%) for thunderstorms across\nthe CWA. Wind shear (30-40kt) is sufficient for some storm \norganization if the updrafts can remain robust in the face of \n~10 deg-C 700mb temps (would need strong forcing to kick-start \nan MCS). \n\nThursday PM: Some models allow the main surface frontal boundary \n(stalled east-west over the upper Great Lakes) to fire convection \nThursday afternoon over the northern half of WI, then plunge it \nsouth into the instability plume (southern half) later in the\nday. Though we've got shower and storm chances (10-35%) to \naccount for this, we'd have to see meaningful forcing for ascent\nthis far south in order to boost our precip chances any higher,\nand right now, we're just not seeing it. \n\nThursday night into Friday AM: A good fraction of available guidance \nhints at a 500mb shortwave descending the eastern crest of the ridge \nThursday night into Friday morning, yielding a round of (potentially \norganized) convection across the state. Exact timing is uncertain, \nbut for now this does yield the strongest wind shear of any feature \non the scope (over 40 kt). If this phases well with lulls / recovery \nfrom the prior round or with the Thursday night LLJ, things could \nget interesting.\n\nSheppard",
